WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.000 --> 00:00:08.469
Have you ever spent a long time thinking about a problem without getting any

2
00:00:08.469 --> 00:00:10.317
closer to a decision?

3
00:00:10.317 --> 00:00:15.779
Sometimes thinking helps us prepare. Sometimes it keeps bringing us back to

4
00:00:15.779 --> 00:00:17.600
the same unanswered question.

5
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:20.945
Imagine you’re getting ready for an appointment.

6
00:00:21.365 --> 00:00:26.245
Checking the address and choosing when to leave are useful planning.

7
00:00:26.245 --> 00:00:32.442
Replaying every possible delay after you’ve made your plan may become a worry

8
00:00:32.442 --> 00:00:32.918
loop.

9
00:00:33.338 --> 00:00:39.308
The difference isn’t simply how long you think. Some problems need time. Ask

10
00:00:39.308 --> 00:00:44.818
whether your thinking is adding information or helping you choose an action.

11
00:00:44.818 --> 00:00:49.972
Try this question: ‘Is there one practical step available to me?’

12
00:00:50.392 --> 00:00:57.317
If there is, make it specific. Set a reminder. Ask a question. Write down when

13
00:00:57.317 --> 00:00:58.702
you’ll handle it.

14
00:00:58.702 --> 00:01:04.717
If there isn’t, you can acknowledge the concern without continuing to solve it

15
00:01:04.717 --> 00:01:05.643
right now.

16
00:01:06.063 --> 00:01:13.413
You might say, ‘I’ve done the planning available to me. I can come back if

17
00:01:13.413 --> 00:01:14.393
something changes.’

18
00:01:14.393 --> 00:01:19.449
Then gently return to the task or person in front of you.

19
00:01:19.869 --> 00:01:26.337
The worry may return. That doesn’t mean you failed. You can notice it and

20
00:01:26.337 --> 00:01:29.572
choose where to place your attention again.
